---
title: Bundle Size and Performance
description: Keep each chart proportional to the marks, scales, host capabilities, and data representation it actually uses.
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Charts is split around capability boundaries. A chart should pay for\nits marks and the specific analytical or spatial primitives it imports, not a\nuniversal chart catalog."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"import-the-narrow-path","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Import the narrow path"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The package root is the ergonomic path for ordinary charts:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The same package provides exact scale subpaths for common numeric and\ncategorical mappings:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"pnpm add @tanstack/charts","lang":"sh"},{"type":"code","value":"import { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scaleOrdinal } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/ordinal'\nimport { scalePoint } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/point'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There is no aggregate "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/scales"},{"type":"text","value":" export. Each exact entry retains only its\nfamily and has no production D3 dependency."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Capability subpaths make optional boundaries explicit:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { mountChart } from '@tanstack/charts/dom'\nimport { mountCanvasChart } from '@tanstack/charts/canvas'\nimport { mountChartRenderer } from '@tanstack/charts/renderer'\nimport { motion } from '@tanstack/charts/motion'\nimport { createChartSpring } from '@tanstack/charts/spring'\nimport { renderChartImage } from '@tanstack/charts/export'\nimport { focusGroupX } from '@tanstack/charts/focus'\nimport { focusGuideX } from '@tanstack/charts/focus/guide'\nimport { brushX } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/brush'\nimport { continuousCursor } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/cursor'\nimport { handleX } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/handle'\nimport { controlledSignal } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/signal'\nimport { zoomX } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/zoom'\nimport { interactiveColorLegend } from '@tanstack/charts/legend'\nimport { keyedSelection, whenSelected } from '@tanstack/charts/selection'\nimport { d3Curve } from '@tanstack/charts/d3/shape'\nimport { tooltip } from '@tanstack/charts/tooltip'\nimport { portal } from '@tanstack/charts/tooltip/portal'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { groupBy } from '@tanstack/charts/transform/group'\nimport { rollingWindow } from '@tanstack/charts/transform/rolling-window'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Canvas is opt-in. The default core and every default framework entry remain\nSVG-based. Canvas enters the module graph only through\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/canvas"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/react/canvas"},{"type":"text","value":", or\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/octane/canvas"},{"type":"text","value":". The React and Octane "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/core"},{"type":"text","value":" entries accept\nan application-supplied renderer without importing Canvas."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Canvas renderer can also be attached to only the dense marks in an\notherwise SVG chart:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { canvasChartRenderer } from '@tanstack/charts/canvas'\n\nlineY(rows, {\n  x: 'time',\n  y: 'value',\n  renderer: canvasChartRenderer,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The shared host includes only the small renderer-selection and layer metadata\ncontract. It does not include the Canvas painter. A consumer that never\nimports the Canvas subpath cannot retain that painter. Measure mixed and\nSVG-only entries separately when reviewing a bundle change."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Non-cartesian geometry is subpath-only:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { pie, polar, radialArc, radialBarRadius } from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\nimport { geoShape } from '@tanstack/charts/geo'\nimport { sunburst } from '@tanstack/charts/hierarchy/sunburst'\nimport { sankeyDiagram } from '@tanstack/charts/network/sankey'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The root entry does not re-export those capabilities. Polar brings in its\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-shape"},{"type":"text","value":" geometry only when the polar subpath is imported; geography does\nthe same for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-geo"},{"type":"text","value":". The exact sunburst entry adds its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-hierarchy"},{"type":"text","value":"\npartition only when imported and reuses the sector geometry shared by ordinary\npolar marks. It does not add hierarchy or sunburst code to ordinary polar\ncharts. Polar value allocation comes from its native "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pie"},{"type":"text","value":" transform.\nThe exact Sankey entry adds "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-sankey"},{"type":"text","value":" and resolved child-mark composition\nonly to Sankey consumers; it does not change root, universal, static force, or\nordinary link bundles.\nImport configured scales, projections, and curve factories from their granular\nD3 modules as the chart requires them. Political boundary data and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"topojson-client"},{"type":"text","value":" remain application dependencies; importing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"geoShape"},{"type":"text","value":" does\nnot bundle an atlas."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tween and spring SVG motion is one optional renderer entry. Importing\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/motion"},{"type":"text","value":" includes both transition models, retained geometry,\nand the SVG reconciler. There is no separate tween-only adapter. The scalar\nphysics sampler remains available independently from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/spring"},{"type":"text","value":".\nCore definitions can contain inert "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"motion"},{"type":"text","value":" policy without importing either\nruntime."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Focus guides are also exact-subpath marks. Importing\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/focus/guide"},{"type":"text","value":" adds renderer-neutral candidate and label\nconstruction, but no DOM host, tooltip, motion runtime, spring solver, React,\nor D3 geometry package."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The controlled-signal snapshot is 0.09 KiB gzip in isolation. The interactive\ncategorical legend, including native DOM controls, adds 2.55 KiB gzip over the\nordinary DOM host. Neither implementation enters root or universal consumers."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controlled keyed selection is also exact-subpath-only. Its semantic-key\ncontroller and post-domain mark filter enter through\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/selection"},{"type":"text","value":"; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"whenSelected"},{"type":"text","value":" reuses the ordinary authored mark\ninstead of importing another geometry or renderer implementation. The root and\nuniversal value entries do not re-export the selection implementation."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The continuous cursor is exact-subpath-only through\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/interaction/cursor"},{"type":"text","value":". It reuses the controlled signal, scale\ninteraction axis, and renderer-neutral guide-node kernel without importing the\ndatum focus guide, tooltip, brush, or a D3 package. Its incremental DOM-host\nfixture adds 3.75 KiB gzip under a 5 KiB cap."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The horizontal scale handle is exact-subpath-only through\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/interaction/handle"},{"type":"text","value":". It reuses the controlled signal,\ncandidate interaction axis, and value-cloning range kernel without importing\ncursor, brush, zoom, guide, or D3 code. Its incremental DOM-host fixture adds\n3.65 KiB gzip under a 5 KiB cap."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Horizontal brushing is exact-subpath-only through\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/interaction/brush"},{"type":"text","value":". It includes the one-dimensional\nscale/snap kernel, DOM host control, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-brush"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-selection"},{"type":"text","value":" only for a\nconsumer that imports it. Root, universal, ordinary DOM, legend, and selection\nconsumers retain none of those modules."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Horizontal zoom is exact-subpath-only through\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/interaction/zoom"},{"type":"text","value":". It includes the controlled semantic-window\nbehavior, final-scale interaction axis, DOM host control, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-zoom"},{"type":"text","value":", and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-selection"},{"type":"text","value":" only for a consumer that imports it. Root, universal, ordinary\nDOM, brush, cursor, legend, and selection consumers retain none of those\nmodules. Its incremental DOM-host fixture adds 20.41 KiB gzip under a 20.55 KiB\ncap."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Your bundler must honor ESM exports and tree shaking. Avoid namespace imports\nwhen a named or subpath import communicates the real dependency."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tooltip rendering is also opt-in. A definition imports "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tooltip"},{"type":"text","value":"; viewport\nlayering additionally imports "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"portal"},{"type":"text","value":" and nests it under the tooltip options:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const interactive = defineChart(definition, {\n  tooltip: {\n    use: tooltip,\n    portal,\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The locked compact React line consumer must remain at or below 31.03 KiB gzip.\nIts retained-module gate rejects tooltip, portal, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-scale"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-format"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-interpolate"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-color"},{"type":"text","value":", transforms, and sibling compact-scale entries.\nSeparate incremental gates limit tooltip and portal growth."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The current locked fixtures measure the compact line scene at 12,726 gzip bytes\nversus 19,890 with D3 linear scales. The equivalent React consumers measure\n31,742 and 38,843 gzip bytes with React and React DOM external. These are\nfixture measurements, not universal savings claims; they show why the compact\nsubset is the normal starting point."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Transforms are root exports for convenience, but their granular subpaths are\nthe smallest contract for reusable preparation code. Ordinary line, compact-\nscale, and tooltip-only bundle fixtures reject every transform module. Each\ntransform family has its own gzip ceiling and rejects unrelated families.\nNumeric and 2D bins intentionally use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-array"},{"type":"text","value":"; row stacking uses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-shape"},{"type":"text","value":";\ngrouping, calendar bins, windows, cumulative values, ranks, normalization,\nselection, and advanced reducers do not retain either dependency."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"add-d3-by-capability","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add D3 by capability"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start with compact scales, then import a granular D3 module when its full\nsemantics fit the chart. Typical upgrade triggers are continuous time or UTC,\nlogarithmic and other transformed scales, piecewise or nonnumeric\ninterpolation, continuous color, curves, specialized transforms, and spatial\nindexes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The upgrade is per scale. A calendar x axis can use D3 while its numeric y axis\nstays compact:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scaleUtc } from 'd3-scale'\n\nconst x = { scale: scaleUtc, nice: true }\nconst y = { scale: scaleLinear, nice: true }","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Declare "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-scale"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@types/d3-scale"},{"type":"text","value":" directly when application source uses\nthat import. A stacked area may add "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-shape"},{"type":"text","value":"; a large nearest-point\ninteraction may add a spatial index. Do not install the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3"},{"type":"text","value":" umbrella for one\ncapability."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The canonical dependency map and official references live in\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../concepts/scales-and-d3.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scales"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"measure-the-complete-feature","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Measure the complete feature"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compare production bundles that render the same behavior:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the same chart family and curve;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the same number and kind of guides;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the same tooltip and keyboard behavior;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the same framework adapter;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the same data preparation;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the same export or spatial capability, when used."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Report raw, gzip, and Brotli sizes. Record the package manager lockfile,\nbundler, minifier, target, and entry source. A root package tarball size or an\nunminified source count is not a user bundle measurement."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"link","href":"../comparison.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"library comparison"}]},{"type":"text","value":" publishes the current pinned\nfour-chart, three-tier bundle snapshot and its limits."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The repository's bundle gates use isolated entries so adding a complex mark\ncannot silently increase the smallest chart. Polar has separate arc-only, pie\nallocation, radial-label, radial-bar, gauge, and scale-backed line/scatter\nceilings; sunburst has an incremental ceiling over the equivalent D3 partition\nkernel; geography has its own projected-shape ceiling. The ordinary line,\nrepresentative-mark, DOM, and framework entries remain exact byte locks."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"separate-preparation-scene-and-paint","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Separate preparation, scene, and paint"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Measure three layers independently:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Data preparation: sorting, grouping, binning, stacking, or layout."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scene build: channels, scales, guides, marks, and focus points."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Surface paint: SVG serialization and keyed reconciliation, or Canvas draw\ncalls, plus optional animation."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This separation reveals whether an expensive chart needs a better encoding, a\nframework-memoized transform, fewer scene nodes, or a different renderer."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"choose-a-sane-representation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose a sane representation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The fastest way to render too much data is to avoid rendering it:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"bin dense distributions;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"aggregate repeated categories;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"use an envelope or sampled line when individual points are not readable;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"restrict a time chart to a controlled visible window;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"use facets only when each panel remains interpretable;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"virtualize application chrome and lanes when only a subset is visible."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every visible SVG node carries DOM and paint cost. Canvas removes the\nper-element DOM cost, but not scene construction, draw work, interaction-point\nmemory, or visual overplotting. More marks are justified only when they\ncommunicate more information."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See "},{"type":"link","href":"./large-data.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Large Data"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for representation thresholds and\ninteraction policies."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"update-efficiently","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Update efficiently"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep fixed definitions at module scope."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Memoize captured-data definitions until their application values change."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reuse derived data references when source data is unchanged."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Let marks infer identity from IDs or unique positions; supply "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":" only when\nthat identity is unavailable or can change."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Memoize expensive derived data in the application."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bound streaming windows."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Build a spatial index only when a measurement justifies it."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disable animation for high-frequency updates or reduced-motion users."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The host reconciles nodes by key and starts interrupted animation from the\ncurrently painted geometry. Stable identity helps both correctness and\nperformance; it does not reduce the cost of an unnecessarily large scene."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"performance-acceptance","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Performance acceptance"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For each supported feature, keep a reproducible gate for:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"cold render time;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"warm update and reorder time;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"resize time;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"node count;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"interaction latency;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"retained heap after repeated mount/update/destroy;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"smallest relevant production bundle."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compare at multiple data sizes and identify the first size where the\nrepresentation itself stops being sensible. Performance claims should name\nthe fixture and percentile, not imply one universal winner."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See "},{"type":"link","href":"./testing-and-debugging.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Testing and Debugging"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for correctness gates\nthat must accompany performance results."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"import-the-narrow-path","text":"Import the narrow path","level":2},{"id":"add-d3-by-capability","text":"Add D3 by capability","level":2},{"id":"measure-the-complete-feature","text":"Measure the complete feature","level":2},{"id":"separate-preparation-scene-and-paint","text":"Separate preparation, scene, and paint","level":2},{"id":"choose-a-sane-representation","text":"Choose a sane representation","level":2},{"id":"update-efficiently","text":"Update efficiently","level":2},{"id":"performance-acceptance","text":"Performance acceptance","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTanStack Charts is split around capability boundaries. A chart should pay for\nits marks and the specific analytical or spatial primitives it imports, not a\nuniversal chart catalog.\n\n## Import the narrow path\n\nThe package root is the ergonomic path for ordinary charts:\n\n```ts\nimport { defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\n```\n\nThe same package provides exact scale subpaths for common numeric and\ncategorical mappings:\n\n```sh\npnpm add @tanstack/charts\n```\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scaleOrdinal } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/ordinal'\nimport { scalePoint } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/point'\n```\n\nThere is no aggregate `/scales` export. Each exact entry retains only its\nfamily and has no production D3 dependency.\n\nCapability subpaths make optional boundaries explicit:\n\n```ts\nimport { mountChart } from '@tanstack/charts/dom'\nimport { mountCanvasChart } from '@tanstack/charts/canvas'\nimport { mountChartRenderer } from '@tanstack/charts/renderer'\nimport { motion } from '@tanstack/charts/motion'\nimport { createChartSpring } from '@tanstack/charts/spring'\nimport { renderChartImage } from '@tanstack/charts/export'\nimport { focusGroupX } from '@tanstack/charts/focus'\nimport { focusGuideX } from '@tanstack/charts/focus/guide'\nimport { brushX } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/brush'\nimport { continuousCursor } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/cursor'\nimport { handleX } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/handle'\nimport { controlledSignal } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/signal'\nimport { zoomX } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/zoom'\nimport { interactiveColorLegend } from '@tanstack/charts/legend'\nimport { keyedSelection, whenSelected } from '@tanstack/charts/selection'\nimport { d3Curve } from '@tanstack/charts/d3/shape'\nimport { tooltip } from '@tanstack/charts/tooltip'\nimport { portal } from '@tanstack/charts/tooltip/portal'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { groupBy } from '@tanstack/charts/transform/group'\nimport { rollingWindow } from '@tanstack/charts/transform/rolling-window'\n```\n\nCanvas is opt-in. The default core and every default framework entry remain\nSVG-based. Canvas enters the module graph only through\n`@tanstack/charts/canvas`, `@tanstack/charts/react/canvas`, or\n`@tanstack/charts/octane/canvas`. The React and Octane `/core` entries accept\nan application-supplied renderer without importing Canvas.\n\nThe Canvas renderer can also be attached to only the dense marks in an\notherwise SVG chart:\n\n```ts\nimport { canvasChartRenderer } from '@tanstack/charts/canvas'\n\nlineY(rows, {\n  x: 'time',\n  y: 'value',\n  renderer: canvasChartRenderer,\n})\n```\n\nThe shared host includes only the small renderer-selection and layer metadata\ncontract. It does not include the Canvas painter. A consumer that never\nimports the Canvas subpath cannot retain that painter. Measure mixed and\nSVG-only entries separately when reviewing a bundle change.\n\nNon-cartesian geometry is subpath-only:\n\n```ts\nimport { pie, polar, radialArc, radialBarRadius } from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\nimport { geoShape } from '@tanstack/charts/geo'\nimport { sunburst } from '@tanstack/charts/hierarchy/sunburst'\nimport { sankeyDiagram } from '@tanstack/charts/network/sankey'\n```\n\nThe root entry does not re-export those capabilities. Polar brings in its\n`d3-shape` geometry only when the polar subpath is imported; geography does\nthe same for `d3-geo`. The exact sunburst entry adds its `d3-hierarchy`\npartition only when imported and reuses the sector geometry shared by ordinary\npolar marks. It does not add hierarchy or sunburst code to ordinary polar\ncharts. Polar value allocation comes from its native `pie` transform.\nThe exact Sankey entry adds `d3-sankey` and resolved child-mark composition\nonly to Sankey consumers; it does not change root, universal, static force, or\nordinary link bundles.\nImport configured scales, projections, and curve factories from their granular\nD3 modules as the chart requires them. Political boundary data and\n`topojson-client` remain application dependencies; importing `geoShape` does\nnot bundle an atlas.\n\nTween and spring SVG motion is one optional renderer entry. Importing\n`@tanstack/charts/motion` includes both transition models, retained geometry,\nand the SVG reconciler. There is no separate tween-only adapter. The scalar\nphysics sampler remains available independently from `@tanstack/charts/spring`.\nCore definitions can contain inert `motion` policy without importing either\nruntime.\n\nFocus guides are also exact-subpath marks. Importing\n`@tanstack/charts/focus/guide` adds renderer-neutral candidate and label\nconstruction, but no DOM host, tooltip, motion runtime, spring solver, React,\nor D3 geometry package.\n\nThe controlled-signal snapshot is 0.09 KiB gzip in isolation. The interactive\ncategorical legend, including native DOM controls, adds 2.55 KiB gzip over the\nordinary DOM host. Neither implementation enters root or universal consumers.\n\nControlled keyed selection is also exact-subpath-only. Its semantic-key\ncontroller and post-domain mark filter enter through\n`@tanstack/charts/selection`; `whenSelected` reuses the ordinary authored mark\ninstead of importing another geometry or renderer implementation. The root and\nuniversal value entries do not re-export the selection implementation.\n\nThe continuous cursor is exact-subpath-only through\n`@tanstack/charts/interaction/cursor`. It reuses the controlled signal, scale\ninteraction axis, and renderer-neutral guide-node kernel without importing the\ndatum focus guide, tooltip, brush, or a D3 package. Its incremental DOM-host\nfixture adds 3.75 KiB gzip under a 5 KiB cap.\n\nThe horizontal scale handle is exact-subpath-only through\n`@tanstack/charts/interaction/handle`. It reuses the controlled signal,\ncandidate interaction axis, and value-cloning range kernel without importing\ncursor, brush, zoom, guide, or D3 code. Its incremental DOM-host fixture adds\n3.65 KiB gzip under a 5 KiB cap.\n\nHorizontal brushing is exact-subpath-only through\n`@tanstack/charts/interaction/brush`. It includes the one-dimensional\nscale/snap kernel, DOM host control, `d3-brush`, and `d3-selection` only for a\nconsumer that imports it. Root, universal, ordinary DOM, legend, and selection\nconsumers retain none of those modules.\n\nHorizontal zoom is exact-subpath-only through\n`@tanstack/charts/interaction/zoom`. It includes the controlled semantic-window\nbehavior, final-scale interaction axis, DOM host control, `d3-zoom`, and\n`d3-selection` only for a consumer that imports it. Root, universal, ordinary\nDOM, brush, cursor, legend, and selection consumers retain none of those\nmodules. Its incremental DOM-host fixture adds 20.41 KiB gzip under a 20.55 KiB\ncap.\n\nYour bundler must honor ESM exports and tree shaking. Avoid namespace imports\nwhen a named or subpath import communicates the real dependency.\n\nTooltip rendering is also opt-in. A definition imports `tooltip`; viewport\nlayering additionally imports `portal` and nests it under the tooltip options:\n\n```ts\nconst interactive = defineChart(definition, {\n  tooltip: {\n    use: tooltip,\n    portal,\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThe locked compact React line consumer must remain at or below 31.03 KiB gzip.\nIts retained-module gate rejects tooltip, portal, `d3-scale`, `d3-format`,\n`d3-interpolate`, `d3-color`, transforms, and sibling compact-scale entries.\nSeparate incremental gates limit tooltip and portal growth.\n\nThe current locked fixtures measure the compact line scene at 12,726 gzip bytes\nversus 19,890 with D3 linear scales. The equivalent React consumers measure\n31,742 and 38,843 gzip bytes with React and React DOM external. These are\nfixture measurements, not universal savings claims; they show why the compact\nsubset is the normal starting point.\n\nTransforms are root exports for convenience, but their granular subpaths are\nthe smallest contract for reusable preparation code. Ordinary line, compact-\nscale, and tooltip-only bundle fixtures reject every transform module. Each\ntransform family has its own gzip ceiling and rejects unrelated families.\nNumeric and 2D bins intentionally use `d3-array`; row stacking uses `d3-shape`;\ngrouping, calendar bins, windows, cumulative values, ranks, normalization,\nselection, and advanced reducers do not retain either dependency.\n\n## Add D3 by capability\n\nStart with compact scales, then import a granular D3 module when its full\nsemantics fit the chart. Typical upgrade triggers are continuous time or UTC,\nlogarithmic and other transformed scales, piecewise or nonnumeric\ninterpolation, continuous color, curves, specialized transforms, and spatial\nindexes.\n\nThe upgrade is per scale. A calendar x axis can use D3 while its numeric y axis\nstays compact:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scaleUtc } from 'd3-scale'\n\nconst x = { scale: scaleUtc, nice: true }\nconst y = { scale: scaleLinear, nice: true }\n```\n\nDeclare `d3-scale` and `@types/d3-scale` directly when application source uses\nthat import. A stacked area may add `d3-shape`; a large nearest-point\ninteraction may add a spatial index. Do not install the `d3` umbrella for one\ncapability.\n\nThe canonical dependency map and official references live in\n[Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md).\n\n## Measure the complete feature\n\nCompare production bundles that render the same behavior:\n\n- the same chart family and curve;\n- the same number and kind of guides;\n- the same tooltip and keyboard behavior;\n- the same framework adapter;\n- the same data preparation;\n- the same export or spatial capability, when used.\n\nReport raw, gzip, and Brotli sizes. Record the package manager lockfile,\nbundler, minifier, target, and entry source. A root package tarball size or an\nunminified source count is not a user bundle measurement.\n\nThe [library comparison](../comparison.md) publishes the current pinned\nfour-chart, three-tier bundle snapshot and its limits.\n\nThe repository's bundle gates use isolated entries so adding a complex mark\ncannot silently increase the smallest chart. Polar has separate arc-only, pie\nallocation, radial-label, radial-bar, gauge, and scale-backed line/scatter\nceilings; sunburst has an incremental ceiling over the equivalent D3 partition\nkernel; geography has its own projected-shape ceiling. The ordinary line,\nrepresentative-mark, DOM, and framework entries remain exact byte locks.\n\n## Separate preparation, scene, and paint\n\nMeasure three layers independently:\n\n1. Data preparation: sorting, grouping, binning, stacking, or layout.\n2. Scene build: channels, scales, guides, marks, and focus points.\n3. Surface paint: SVG serialization and keyed reconciliation, or Canvas draw\n   calls, plus optional animation.\n\nThis separation reveals whether an expensive chart needs a better encoding, a\nframework-memoized transform, fewer scene nodes, or a different renderer.\n\n## Choose a sane representation\n\nThe fastest way to render too much data is to avoid rendering it:\n\n- bin dense distributions;\n- aggregate repeated categories;\n- use an envelope or sampled line when individual points are not readable;\n- restrict a time chart to a controlled visible window;\n- use facets only when each panel remains interpretable;\n- virtualize application chrome and lanes when only a subset is visible.\n\nEvery visible SVG node carries DOM and paint cost. Canvas removes the\nper-element DOM cost, but not scene construction, draw work, interaction-point\nmemory, or visual overplotting. More marks are justified only when they\ncommunicate more information.\n\nSee [Large Data](./large-data.md) for representation thresholds and\ninteraction policies.\n\n## Update efficiently\n\n- Keep fixed definitions at module scope.\n- Memoize captured-data definitions until their application values change.\n- Reuse derived data references when source data is unchanged.\n- Let marks infer identity from IDs or unique positions; supply `key` only when\n  that identity is unavailable or can change.\n- Memoize expensive derived data in the application.\n- Bound streaming windows.\n- Build a spatial index only when a measurement justifies it.\n- Disable animation for high-frequency updates or reduced-motion users.\n\nThe host reconciles nodes by key and starts interrupted animation from the\ncurrently painted geometry. Stable identity helps both correctness and\nperformance; it does not reduce the cost of an unnecessarily large scene.\n\n## Performance acceptance\n\nFor each supported feature, keep a reproducible gate for:\n\n- cold render time;\n- warm update and reorder time;\n- resize time;\n- node count;\n- interaction latency;\n- retained heap after repeated mount/update/destroy;\n- smallest relevant production bundle.\n\nCompare at multiple data sizes and identify the first size where the\nrepresentation itself stops being sensible. Performance claims should name\nthe fixture and percentile, not imply one universal winner.\n\nSee [Testing and Debugging](./testing-and-debugging.md) for correctness gates\nthat must accompany performance results.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Bundle Size and Performance\ndescription: Keep each chart proportional to the marks, scales, host capabilities, and data representation it actually uses.\n---\n\nTanStack Charts is split around capability boundaries. A chart should pay for\nits marks and the specific analytical or spatial primitives it imports, not a\nuniversal chart catalog.\n\n## Import the narrow path\n\nThe package root is the ergonomic path for ordinary charts:\n\n```ts\nimport { defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\n```\n\nThe same package provides exact scale subpaths for common numeric and\ncategorical mappings:\n\n```sh\npnpm add @tanstack/charts\n```\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scaleOrdinal } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/ordinal'\nimport { scalePoint } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/point'\n```\n\nThere is no aggregate `/scales` export. Each exact entry retains only its\nfamily and has no production D3 dependency.\n\nCapability subpaths make optional boundaries explicit:\n\n```ts\nimport { mountChart } from '@tanstack/charts/dom'\nimport { mountCanvasChart } from '@tanstack/charts/canvas'\nimport { mountChartRenderer } from '@tanstack/charts/renderer'\nimport { motion } from '@tanstack/charts/motion'\nimport { createChartSpring } from '@tanstack/charts/spring'\nimport { renderChartImage } from '@tanstack/charts/export'\nimport { focusGroupX } from '@tanstack/charts/focus'\nimport { focusGuideX } from '@tanstack/charts/focus/guide'\nimport { brushX } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/brush'\nimport { continuousCursor } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/cursor'\nimport { handleX } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/handle'\nimport { controlledSignal } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/signal'\nimport { zoomX } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/zoom'\nimport { interactiveColorLegend } from '@tanstack/charts/legend'\nimport { keyedSelection, whenSelected } from '@tanstack/charts/selection'\nimport { d3Curve } from '@tanstack/charts/d3/shape'\nimport { tooltip } from '@tanstack/charts/tooltip'\nimport { portal } from '@tanstack/charts/tooltip/portal'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { groupBy } from '@tanstack/charts/transform/group'\nimport { rollingWindow } from '@tanstack/charts/transform/rolling-window'\n```\n\nCanvas is opt-in. The default core and every default framework entry remain\nSVG-based. Canvas enters the module graph only through\n`@tanstack/charts/canvas`, `@tanstack/charts/react/canvas`, or\n`@tanstack/charts/octane/canvas`. The React and Octane `/core` entries accept\nan application-supplied renderer without importing Canvas.\n\nThe Canvas renderer can also be attached to only the dense marks in an\notherwise SVG chart:\n\n```ts\nimport { canvasChartRenderer } from '@tanstack/charts/canvas'\n\nlineY(rows, {\n  x: 'time',\n  y: 'value',\n  renderer: canvasChartRenderer,\n})\n```\n\nThe shared host includes only the small renderer-selection and layer metadata\ncontract. It does not include the Canvas painter. A consumer that never\nimports the Canvas subpath cannot retain that painter. Measure mixed and\nSVG-only entries separately when reviewing a bundle change.\n\nNon-cartesian geometry is subpath-only:\n\n```ts\nimport { pie, polar, radialArc, radialBarRadius } from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\nimport { geoShape } from '@tanstack/charts/geo'\nimport { sunburst } from '@tanstack/charts/hierarchy/sunburst'\nimport { sankeyDiagram } from '@tanstack/charts/network/sankey'\n```\n\nThe root entry does not re-export those capabilities. Polar brings in its\n`d3-shape` geometry only when the polar subpath is imported; geography does\nthe same for `d3-geo`. The exact sunburst entry adds its `d3-hierarchy`\npartition only when imported and reuses the sector geometry shared by ordinary\npolar marks. It does not add hierarchy or sunburst code to ordinary polar\ncharts. Polar value allocation comes from its native `pie` transform.\nThe exact Sankey entry adds `d3-sankey` and resolved child-mark composition\nonly to Sankey consumers; it does not change root, universal, static force, or\nordinary link bundles.\nImport configured scales, projections, and curve factories from their granular\nD3 modules as the chart requires them. Political boundary data and\n`topojson-client` remain application dependencies; importing `geoShape` does\nnot bundle an atlas.\n\nTween and spring SVG motion is one optional renderer entry. Importing\n`@tanstack/charts/motion` includes both transition models, retained geometry,\nand the SVG reconciler. There is no separate tween-only adapter. The scalar\nphysics sampler remains available independently from `@tanstack/charts/spring`.\nCore definitions can contain inert `motion` policy without importing either\nruntime.\n\nFocus guides are also exact-subpath marks. Importing\n`@tanstack/charts/focus/guide` adds renderer-neutral candidate and label\nconstruction, but no DOM host, tooltip, motion runtime, spring solver, React,\nor D3 geometry package.\n\nThe controlled-signal snapshot is 0.09 KiB gzip in isolation. The interactive\ncategorical legend, including native DOM controls, adds 2.55 KiB gzip over the\nordinary DOM host. Neither implementation enters root or universal consumers.\n\nControlled keyed selection is also exact-subpath-only. Its semantic-key\ncontroller and post-domain mark filter enter through\n`@tanstack/charts/selection`; `whenSelected` reuses the ordinary authored mark\ninstead of importing another geometry or renderer implementation. The root and\nuniversal value entries do not re-export the selection implementation.\n\nThe continuous cursor is exact-subpath-only through\n`@tanstack/charts/interaction/cursor`. It reuses the controlled signal, scale\ninteraction axis, and renderer-neutral guide-node kernel without importing the\ndatum focus guide, tooltip, brush, or a D3 package. Its incremental DOM-host\nfixture adds 3.75 KiB gzip under a 5 KiB cap.\n\nThe horizontal scale handle is exact-subpath-only through\n`@tanstack/charts/interaction/handle`. It reuses the controlled signal,\ncandidate interaction axis, and value-cloning range kernel without importing\ncursor, brush, zoom, guide, or D3 code. Its incremental DOM-host fixture adds\n3.65 KiB gzip under a 5 KiB cap.\n\nHorizontal brushing is exact-subpath-only through\n`@tanstack/charts/interaction/brush`. It includes the one-dimensional\nscale/snap kernel, DOM host control, `d3-brush`, and `d3-selection` only for a\nconsumer that imports it. Root, universal, ordinary DOM, legend, and selection\nconsumers retain none of those modules.\n\nHorizontal zoom is exact-subpath-only through\n`@tanstack/charts/interaction/zoom`. It includes the controlled semantic-window\nbehavior, final-scale interaction axis, DOM host control, `d3-zoom`, and\n`d3-selection` only for a consumer that imports it. Root, universal, ordinary\nDOM, brush, cursor, legend, and selection consumers retain none of those\nmodules. Its incremental DOM-host fixture adds 20.41 KiB gzip under a 20.55 KiB\ncap.\n\nYour bundler must honor ESM exports and tree shaking. Avoid namespace imports\nwhen a named or subpath import communicates the real dependency.\n\nTooltip rendering is also opt-in. A definition imports `tooltip`; viewport\nlayering additionally imports `portal` and nests it under the tooltip options:\n\n```ts\nconst interactive = defineChart(definition, {\n  tooltip: {\n    use: tooltip,\n    portal,\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThe locked compact React line consumer must remain at or below 31.03 KiB gzip.\nIts retained-module gate rejects tooltip, portal, `d3-scale`, `d3-format`,\n`d3-interpolate`, `d3-color`, transforms, and sibling compact-scale entries.\nSeparate incremental gates limit tooltip and portal growth.\n\nThe current locked fixtures measure the compact line scene at 12,726 gzip bytes\nversus 19,890 with D3 linear scales. The equivalent React consumers measure\n31,742 and 38,843 gzip bytes with React and React DOM external. These are\nfixture measurements, not universal savings claims; they show why the compact\nsubset is the normal starting point.\n\nTransforms are root exports for convenience, but their granular subpaths are\nthe smallest contract for reusable preparation code. Ordinary line, compact-\nscale, and tooltip-only bundle fixtures reject every transform module. Each\ntransform family has its own gzip ceiling and rejects unrelated families.\nNumeric and 2D bins intentionally use `d3-array`; row stacking uses `d3-shape`;\ngrouping, calendar bins, windows, cumulative values, ranks, normalization,\nselection, and advanced reducers do not retain either dependency.\n\n## Add D3 by capability\n\nStart with compact scales, then import a granular D3 module when its full\nsemantics fit the chart. Typical upgrade triggers are continuous time or UTC,\nlogarithmic and other transformed scales, piecewise or nonnumeric\ninterpolation, continuous color, curves, specialized transforms, and spatial\nindexes.\n\nThe upgrade is per scale. A calendar x axis can use D3 while its numeric y axis\nstays compact:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scaleUtc } from 'd3-scale'\n\nconst x = { scale: scaleUtc, nice: true }\nconst y = { scale: scaleLinear, nice: true }\n```\n\nDeclare `d3-scale` and `@types/d3-scale` directly when application source uses\nthat import. A stacked area may add `d3-shape`; a large nearest-point\ninteraction may add a spatial index. Do not install the `d3` umbrella for one\ncapability.\n\nThe canonical dependency map and official references live in\n[Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md).\n\n## Measure the complete feature\n\nCompare production bundles that render the same behavior:\n\n- the same chart family and curve;\n- the same number and kind of guides;\n- the same tooltip and keyboard behavior;\n- the same framework adapter;\n- the same data preparation;\n- the same export or spatial capability, when used.\n\nReport raw, gzip, and Brotli sizes. Record the package manager lockfile,\nbundler, minifier, target, and entry source. A root package tarball size or an\nunminified source count is not a user bundle measurement.\n\nThe [library comparison](../comparison.md) publishes the current pinned\nfour-chart, three-tier bundle snapshot and its limits.\n\nThe repository's bundle gates use isolated entries so adding a complex mark\ncannot silently increase the smallest chart. Polar has separate arc-only, pie\nallocation, radial-label, radial-bar, gauge, and scale-backed line/scatter\nceilings; sunburst has an incremental ceiling over the equivalent D3 partition\nkernel; geography has its own projected-shape ceiling. The ordinary line,\nrepresentative-mark, DOM, and framework entries remain exact byte locks.\n\n## Separate preparation, scene, and paint\n\nMeasure three layers independently:\n\n1. Data preparation: sorting, grouping, binning, stacking, or layout.\n2. Scene build: channels, scales, guides, marks, and focus points.\n3. Surface paint: SVG serialization and keyed reconciliation, or Canvas draw\n   calls, plus optional animation.\n\nThis separation reveals whether an expensive chart needs a better encoding, a\nframework-memoized transform, fewer scene nodes, or a different renderer.\n\n## Choose a sane representation\n\nThe fastest way to render too much data is to avoid rendering it:\n\n- bin dense distributions;\n- aggregate repeated categories;\n- use an envelope or sampled line when individual points are not readable;\n- restrict a time chart to a controlled visible window;\n- use facets only when each panel remains interpretable;\n- virtualize application chrome and lanes when only a subset is visible.\n\nEvery visible SVG node carries DOM and paint cost. Canvas removes the\nper-element DOM cost, but not scene construction, draw work, interaction-point\nmemory, or visual overplotting. More marks are justified only when they\ncommunicate more information.\n\nSee [Large Data](./large-data.md) for representation thresholds and\ninteraction policies.\n\n## Update efficiently\n\n- Keep fixed definitions at module scope.\n- Memoize captured-data definitions until their application values change.\n- Reuse derived data references when source data is unchanged.\n- Let marks infer identity from IDs or unique positions; supply `key` only when\n  that identity is unavailable or can change.\n- Memoize expensive derived data in the application.\n- Bound streaming windows.\n- Build a spatial index only when a measurement justifies it.\n- Disable animation for high-frequency updates or reduced-motion users.\n\nThe host reconciles nodes by key and starts interrupted animation from the\ncurrently painted geometry. Stable identity helps both correctness and\nperformance; it does not reduce the cost of an unnecessarily large scene.\n\n## Performance acceptance\n\nFor each supported feature, keep a reproducible gate for:\n\n- cold render time;\n- warm update and reorder time;\n- resize time;\n- node count;\n- interaction latency;\n- retained heap after repeated mount/update/destroy;\n- smallest relevant production bundle.\n\nCompare at multiple data sizes and identify the first size where the\nrepresentation itself stops being sensible. Performance claims should name\nthe fixture and percentile, not imply one universal winner.\n\nSee [Testing and Debugging](./testing-and-debugging.md) for correctness gates\nthat must accompany performance results.\n"}
